/// Rewrite every RELATIVE import in a lifted module to its `@/…` equivalent.
///
/// A file and a record resolve imports differently, and this is the whole difference. `./planningTerminal`
/// works in a FILE because Vite resolves it against the file's directory. A RECORD is a string in a store
/// with no directory at all, so the runtime loader can only match absolute `@/…` specifiers against the
/// platform registry or another record's `provides` — `platformBoundary.test.ts` states the rule outright:
/// records must import absolutely. Lifting a module verbatim therefore produces a record that cannot load
/// AND takes its importing page down with it (`planningpage`, `githubpage` and `mcppage` all failed this
/// way; 12 of the first 18 modules tried were affected, one of them with 15 relative imports).
///
/// A specifier that resolves to no file on disk is LEFT ALONE. Rewriting it would be a guess, and a wrong
/// `@/…` is worse than an honest `./…`: the loader names an unresolved absolute specifier, while a guessed
/// one may silently resolve to the wrong module.
/// Rewrite a Rust module's RELATIVE `include_str!` / `include_bytes!` paths so they survive
/// materialization.
///
/// `include_str!("../data/x.json")` resolves relative to the FILE THAT CONTAINS IT. Materialized,
/// that file is `target/debug/build/<crate>-<hash>/out/crate.rs`, so the same relative path points
/// somewhere else entirely and the build fails with "couldn't read" — measured on `bsc-sound`, which
/// is why the migration tool refused these crates rather than predicting it.
///
/// The rewrite happens HERE, at harvest, so the stored record is the thing that compiles. Repairing
/// it in `build.rs` instead would mean a build script silently fixing up a record that does not build
/// on its own — a second copy of the truth, which is the failure mode this whole migration exists to
/// remove.
///
/// `CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR` is set for the crate being compiled and `include!` does not change that, so
/// `<manifest>/<the file's dir relative to the manifest>/<original path>` names exactly what the
/// relative path meant before.
///
/// `..` SEGMENTS ARE NORMALIZED, and leaving them in was a real bug. The joined path is anchored at the
/// module's OWN directory, which this migration then DELETES — so `src/session/settings.rs`'s
/// `include_str!("../../data/x.json")` became `<manifest>/src/session/../../data/x.json`, and no
/// filesystem resolves `..` through a directory that is gone. Three calls in
/// `src-tauri/src/session/settings.rs` broke exactly this way. The normalization is purely LEXICAL over
/// two things known at harvest time — a real relative directory and a literal — so it cannot be the
/// off-by-one this comment used to warn about: nothing here depends on the compile-time value, only on
/// the text that is prepended to it. A `..` that would climb ABOVE the manifest is kept, because the
/// manifest's own parent is a directory that still exists.
///
/// Left alone: an already-absolutized `concat!(…)` form, a path that is not a plain string literal,
/// and — the reason this reuses [`CodeMask`] — a token that is not CODE.
///
/// That last one is the same blind spot [`crate::migrate::include_str_targets`] was moved off raw
/// text for on 2026-09-04, and it lands harder here. A reporter that miscounts a token in a comment
/// refuses a crate, loudly. A REWRITER that miscounts one edits it: the `//` prose above became
/// `concat!(env!("CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR"), …)`, and a fixture STRING naming `include_str!` — which is
/// exactly what `migrate.rs` carries for the tests OF the refusal rule — had its CONTENTS changed,
/// so the test asserting on that fixture silently started asserting something else. There is now ONE
/// answer to "is this token code", not two that can disagree.
/// A relative path with its `.` and `..` segments resolved TEXTUALLY.
///
/// The anchor directory a rewritten `include_str!` is joined to is deleted by the migration, so a `..`
/// left in the text has nothing to walk back through. Resolving it here is safe precisely because both
/// halves are known now: `src/session` is a real directory relative to the manifest and the literal is
/// the author's own path.
///
/// A `..` that cannot be cancelled is KEPT rather than dropped — dropping it would silently change
/// which file is read, and `<manifest>/../sibling` is a path that resolves.
pub(crate) fn normalize_rel(path: &str) -> String {
    let mut out: Vec<&str> = Vec::new();
    for seg in path.split('/') {
        match seg {
            "" | "." => {}
            ".." if out.last().is_some_and(|l| *l != "..") => {
                out.pop();
            }
            s => out.push(s),
        }
    }
    out.join("/")
}
